 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy: 2Q (Selected)
+
+The preview worker uses a 2Q eviction policy with two queues:
+
+- **A1 (First-Use Queue)**: Newly admitted entries enter A1 at the head. On access, an entry graduates to Am. When A1 exceeds its capacity, the tail entry is evicted.
+- **Am (Frequent-Use Queue)**: Entries that have been accessed at least once reside here. On access, the entry moves to the head. When Am exceeds its capacity, the tail entry is evicted.
+
+Queue sizing: the 12 GiB cache budget is split 25% / 75% between A1 and Am (3 GiB / 9 GiB). This ratio is a tunable parameter; the split protects the comparison-sheet working set in Am from being displaced by one-off screening scans that only touch A1.
+
+Admission: when a render completes, the new sheet is admitted to A1. Before admission, evict from A1 tail until A1 is within its budget; if A1 is already within budget, no eviction occurs.
+
+Graduation: on any cache hit (request for a cached key), if the entry resides in A1 it is moved to the head of Am (creating the entry in Am if needed, evicting from Am tail if Am would exceed its budget). Hits on entries already in Am move them to the head of Am.
+
+Replacement: A1 and Am each enforce their own capacity independently. The two queues share the single 12 GiB budget via the 25/75 split. Total cached JPEG data never exceeds 12 GiB.
+
+All eviction bookkeeping (queue pointers, sizes, graduation logic) is private to the preview worker. The external interface (`GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}`), render queue concurrency (max 4), deduplication map, and disk budget are unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (Two-Queue) | LRU, Frequency-based with aging | 2Q protects the repeatedly accessed comparison-sheet working set in Am from being displaced by one-off sequential screening scans that only touch A1. The 25/75 split (3 GiB / 9 GiB) favors retention of reused sheets. The extra bookkeeping (two queues, graduation logic, split budget) is accepted as the cost of scan resistance. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy selected as 2Q with 25/75 A1/Am split.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Whether the 25/75 split should be configurable at runtime or remain a compile-time constant.
+2. Whether to instrument queue residency metrics for operational tuning.
